// The DRIVERS' report path — the erased sink, kept OUT of diagnostics.npk.
//
// Why its own module: `REACH` is IMPORT-scoped, so an async function anywhere in
// a module makes `DeadlineExceeded` reachable in every program that imports it —
// an async body can be CANCELLED, which is also why TYPE-037 refuses
// `async` + `never fails`. Putting these three functions in diagnostics.npk made
// twelve of the compiler's OWN unit tests grow a `(DeadlineExceeded)` arm,
// including `tests/backend/ir_expr.npk`, which does not mention the report path
// at all. An arm acknowledging something that cannot happen in that program is
// an acknowledgement worth less than none — the exhaustive `failsafe` (D-179)
// earns its keep by being TRUE, so the fix is to put the async surface where it
// belongs rather than to spend the rule's credibility.
//
// diagnostics.npk keeps what it always was: the list, the diagnostic, and
// `diag_line`, the ONE formatter (1.0.8). This file is the one WRITER --
// hence `diag_writer`, since a module may not share a name with a function
// it declares and `diag_report` is the walk's own name.

mod:diag_writer;

use "./diagnostics.npk".*;
use "./source_location.npk".*;
use "./source.npk".*;


// HOW LONG A DIAGNOSTIC WRITE MAY TAKE. The std descriptors stay BLOCKING --
// the one stated D-071 exception (1.1.12c) -- so this bounds a pathological
// pipe reader rather than an ordinary write, and it is generous on purpose: a
// report that gives up is a defect the reader never learns about.
fixed Duration:DIAG_WITHIN = Duration{ ns: 5000000000i64 };

// THE ONE DIAGNOSTIC WALK -- GENERIC AND BORROWING (D-229). Five copies of
// this loop existed -- `report` in src/main.npk, `report` in tools/check.npk,
// and three inline walks across resolve_check and parse_check -- and the 1.0.8
// note further up this file records what letting a second copy exist costs.
//
// IT WAS `dyn Writer` UNTIL D-229, AND THE ERASURE HAD TO GO. D-075 routed
// diagnostics through a trait object so `npkg test` could capture and compare
// them; a `dyn` coercion MOVES, so the capture buffer is consumed by the call
// and cannot be read back -- which is precisely what capture must do. Capture
// needs the sink to reference storage the CALLER retains, and every sanctioned
// way to reference external state from inside an owned `dyn` is now closed: a
// channel endpoint is refused at the coercion (D-215), a borrow inside an
// erased value is D-207's own stated hazard, and a `shared_arena` slot is
// written once before its handle escapes (D-154). D-075's premise predates all
// three and did not survive the language's own hardening.
//
// A BOUND PARAMETER TAKEN BY POINTER gives back exactly what erasure took: the
// caller keeps the writer and can read it afterwards. What the erasure was
// protecting -- ONE walk in source, which `check_one_renderer` pins -- the
// generic keeps, because the five-copies problem was source duplication and
// never instance count. It is also the verification-friendlier shape:
// monomorphized direct calls rather than a vtable indirection.
//
// IT SORTS (D-229). `diaglist_render` always did and the driver path never
// did; one walk has one ordering. The argument is not reproducibility -- the
// driver path is already deterministic run to run -- it is that sorting makes
// the output a function of the diagnostic SET rather than of pass discovery
// order, so 1.4.8's capture goldens survive a future pass reordering. The sort
// is stable, so cause-before-consequence at one span is preserved.
//
// BEST-EFFORT BY THE TIME IT PRINTS, deliberately (D-163's reasoned `?|`): a
// broken stderr cannot be reported on stderr, so each write swallows its own
// Result rather than inventing a failure nobody can act on.
// NOT `never fails`, and it cannot be: TYPE-037 refuses the pair, because a
// suspended task can be CANCELLED and cancellation is an error the contract
// would be denying (D-163). The best-effort promise this walk actually keeps is
// per-write -- each `?|` below -- rather than a signature-level one.
pub async func:diag_report<W: Writer> = NIL(W->:sink, DiagList->:list,
                                            SourceManager->:sm, Duration:within) {
    drop diaglist_sort(list);
    int64:n = raw diaglist_count(list);
    int64:d = 0i64;
    while (d < n) {
        string:ln = string_concat(raw diag_line(raw diaglist_at(list, d), sm),
                                  "\n");
        discard(await sink.write(string_bytes(ln), within) ?| 0i64);
        d = d + 1i64;
    }
    if (list.overflowed) {
        string:ov = string_concat(raw diag_overflow_line(), "\n");
        discard(await sink.write(string_bytes(ov), within) ?| 0i64);
    }
    await sink.flush(within) ?| NIL;
    pass NIL;
};

// The drivers' one-line spelling. The sink is BORROWED now, so the "construct
// stderr once" shape the D-209 note wanted is available again in principle --
// but each reporting site is still its own early exit, so each builds its own
// and the dup it owns is closed by the `OwnedFd` drop at this function's exit.
pub async func:diag_report_stderr = NIL(DiagList->:list, SourceManager->:sm) {
    TextWriter<ByteWriter>:tw = relay std_err();
    await diag_report(@tw, list, sm, DIAG_WITHIN) ?| NIL;
    pass NIL;
};

// A driver's own message -- "cannot write the output file" -- through the SAME
// sink as the diagnostics. Two paths to one stream is the thing the blueprint
// philosophy rejects, and it is what this file's own 1.0.8 note is about. No
// erasure here either: there was never a reason for one, and D-229 removed the
// spelling that made it look required.
pub async func:diag_say = NIL(string:msg) {
    TextWriter<ByteWriter>:tw = relay std_err();
    string:ln = string_concat(msg, "\n");
    discard(await tw.write(string_bytes(ln), DIAG_WITHIN) ?| 0i64);
    await tw.flush(DIAG_WITHIN) ?| NIL;
    pass NIL;
};
